  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Praise-oratory of the imbongi (plural iimbongi), the Xhosa praise-poet of the Eastern Cape of South Africa, who delivers izibongo — improvised, high-energy declamatory praises of chiefs, ancestors and, in the modern era, political leaders — often clad in animal skins and carrying spears, appearing at installations, funerals and public gatherings. A living tradition documented by scholars such as Jeff Opland and embodied by figures like S.E.K. Mqhayi and, in the democratic era, the praise-poet at Nelson Mandela's 1994 inauguration. Delivery is rapid, loud, rhythmically-surging spoken praise, rich in metaphor and delivered in a single sustained breath-driven flow.",
